Dynamic bottleneck prediction can warn manufacturers of emerging throughput constraints and guide resource adjustment before production is disrupted.
This study addresses not only which resource and station will become the bottleneck, but also when the event will begin, how long it will persist, what observable process condition is associated with it, and how much order-processing time remains.
Existing studies mainly rely on single-entity data returned by machine or workstation sensors, with limited representation of workers, materials, buffers, and logistics and of their relations.
This narrow observation scope leads to incomplete factory-graph modelling, reduces prediction accuracy, and makes it difficult to capture disturbances that propagate across resources after a delay.
To address these limitations, we propose the \textbf{Process-Informed Heterogeneous-Graph Spatiotemporal Transformer with Delayed Propagation (PHGST-DP)}.
A disturbance-controlled digital production line provides traceable multi-resource trajectories, while process-informed rules and persistence filtering construct sustained bottleneck events and process-cause targets; deterministic cause-aligned regime identifiers provide additional operational context.
PHGST-DP then combines a heterogeneous process--logistics graph with a delay-pattern module that clusters short temporal patches into recurrent prototypes and uses them to condition cross-resource interaction, thereby associating earlier resource states with later constraints.
The model jointly reports the bottleneck station, onset, duration, process cause, and remaining order time.
Unified-sample comparisons and ablations show PHGST-DP leads event identification, early warning, state tracking, and duration estimation, linking gains to cross-resource modelling, process-informed supervision, and delay-pattern conditioning; report generation fits production update intervals on tested hardware.
These results validate PHGST-DP under the studied digital-twin conditions and support production warning and decision-making for resource rescheduling, material replenishment, equipment maintenance, and logistics adjustment.

\zhpara{动态瓶颈预测能够在吞吐约束影响生产之前发出预警，并为资源调整和生产干预提供依据，对实际生产具有重要意义。
本文研究的不仅是未来哪一资源、哪一工位会成为瓶颈，还包括瓶颈何时开始、持续多久、与何种可观测生产状态相关，以及订单尚需多长时间完成。
现有研究主要关注机器或工位传感器返回的单一实体数据，对人员、物料、缓冲区和物流等其他资源实体及其相互联系表征不足。
这种观测范围使工厂图建模不充分，降低预测准确性，也难以刻画扰动经过一定时延后在不同资源之间传播并形成瓶颈的过程。
为解决上述问题，本文提出\textbf{面向延迟传播的过程启发式异构图时空 Transformer（PHGST-DP）}。
可控扰动的数字化生产线提供可追溯的多资源轨迹，过程启发式规则与持续性过滤构造持续性瓶颈事件和过程原因目标，确定性生成的原因对齐工况标识则提供额外运营上下文。
在此基础上，PHGST-DP 将异构工艺—物流图与延迟模式模块结合起来；该模块将短时间片段聚类为重复模式原型，并利用这些原型调节跨资源交互，从而将较早出现的资源状态与随后形成的瓶颈联系起来。
模型最终联合输出瓶颈工位、开始时间、持续时间、过程原因和订单剩余时间。
统一测试样本上的多模型比较与消融实验表明，PHGST-DP 在事件识别、提前预警、状态跟踪和持续时间估计方面表现最优，其优势与异构跨资源建模、过程启发式监督和延迟模式条件化相关；同时，测试硬件上的报告生成耗时满足生产更新周期。
上述结果验证了 PHGST-DP 在所研究数字孪生条件下的有效性，可为实际生产中的资源重调度、物料补充、设备维护和物流调整提供预警与决策支持。}
